		{"id":"b5b638a4633db5e5","type":"group","x":2080,"y":2440,"width":1453,"height":680,"label":"Legislative Response to Homlessness and Hostile Design"},
		{"id":"d177c7985935a88d","type":"group","x":641,"y":2780,"width":1200,"height":1080,"label":"Safer Bathrooms"},
		{"id":"f2ef4d59825f42c1","type":"group","x":-1360,"y":-1360,"width":4480,"height":560,"label":"Filming Stage"},
		{"id":"f77f8cfacf803442","type":"group","x":640,"y":-1260,"width":1230,"height":387,"label":"Examples"},
		{"id":"9e8a910c0da587f1","type":"group","x":1891,"y":-1260,"width":1149,"height":387,"label":"Alternatives"},
		{"id":"24c03b478059f673","type":"group","x":-1200,"y":-320,"width":1077,"height":360,"label":"Research Stage"},
		{"id":"0f7b45ea2fae5e38","type":"text","text":"# Goals\n\n1. Understand what is Hostile Architecture.\n2. Find real-life examples and take photos of these.\n3. Consider interviewing/getting primary accounts from rough sleepers.","x":-588,"y":-286,"width":388,"height":266},
		{"id":"c12af16df22015e0","type":"text","text":"# Resources\n\n- [[Invisible Women]].\n\t- [[there is a war on public toilets]]\n\t- [[little thing, big consequence]] - blue lights reminided me of this idea.","x":-1160,"y":-286,"width":540,"height":266},
		{"id":"f9b1ebd3771f9f4e","type":"text","text":"\n> [!question] Questions\n> **What** is hostile architecture.  \n> **Who** is most impacted by it.  \n> **Where** did it originate.  \n> **How**  \n> **Why** is it implemented instead of a different solution.\n\n\n> [!danger] Arguments\n> - Hostile solutions are not only unethical but they are also ineffective. There are better alternatives.\n\n\n> [!tip] Conclusions\n> - Hostile Architecture solutions can at first sound effective, however, when you look deeper there are inadvertent harmful side-effects (at it seems like that is always true). \n\n\n> [!cite] Sources\n> I'll mark these in yellow.\n> - [A qualitative study of the perceived effects of blue lights in washrooms on people who use injection drugs | Harm Reduction Journal | Full Text (biomedcentral.com)](https://harmreductionjournal.biomedcentral.com/articles/10.1186/1477-7517-10-22)\n\n\n> [!success] Insight\n> Unique thoughts that I can argument well and are supported by sources. In other words, my own ideas that make sense.\n\n\n> [!bug] Issues / Concerns\n> - Define hostile architecture. For now it feels quite circular. If I select \"hostile architecture\" as anything that badly solves social issues, then the criticism is biased against it. \n> \t- I either have to only criticised the bad implementations of \"hostile architecture\", and mention the good ones.\n> \t- Or show that it fails on a fundamental level, meaning I have to give a clear definition of that which I criticise (and call \"hostile architecture\"),","x":-1378,"y":141,"width":500,"height":1427},
		{"id":"f5873340e30a27b7","type":"file","file":"Thoughts/37 Interests/2 Writing/Blog/Drafts/Hostile Architecture/Hostile Architecture.md","x":-2118,"y":141,"width":640,"height":1209},
		{"id":"0ed2573b7d127a22","type":"text","text":"# Disperse\n\n- policing\n- changes to built environment (\"hostile architecture\")","x":-878,"y":1760,"width":250,"height":240},
		{"id":"3fc7e4d1db06a75e","type":"text","text":"# Support\n\n- injection sites\n- housing provision","x":-878,"y":2032,"width":250,"height":189},
		{"id":"c9b467b2bf9b91e6","type":"text","text":"# Injection of Drugs in Public Spaces\n\n> Strategies to reduce public injection drug use, or to reduce the harms associated with injecting in public, could benefit people who use injection drugs and the communities where they live.^[[A qualitative study of the perceived effects of blue lights in washrooms on people who use injection drugs | Harm Reduction Journal | Full Text (biomedcentral.com)](https://harmreductionjournal.biomedcentral.com/articles/10.1186/1477-7517-10-22)]","x":-436,"y":1584,"width":356,"height":496},
		{"id":"057ff7e94ce80619","type":"text","text":"> [!note]\n> I'm considering turning this essay into a documentary once I have the essay part finished, have better understanding of the topic, and if still have the desire to translate it into a video format.","x":-1320,"y":-1320,"width":330,"height":313},
		{"id":"1d67efc88553f59c","type":"text","text":"[['Hostile Architecture' as a dispersal strategy to reduce 'displays of anti-social behaviour']]^[Not convinced by this phrasing].\n","x":-436,"y":720,"width":356,"height":236,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"a80dddac66201173","type":"text","text":"Hostile Architecture, in principle prioritises control over inclusivity, at the expense of those vulnerable and defenseless.\n\n![[Control outweighs Inclusivity graphic.png]]","x":-436,"y":141,"width":356,"height":512},
		{"id":"49708ccb3bfe9fad","type":"text","text":"> [!question] What is Hostile Architecture trying to solve? #question \n\nHostile Architecture is a response to societal issues like homelessness. illegal drug-use, violent street crime, etc.\n\n>[!danger] It tries and fails.\n>These design implementations are a misunderstanding of how to treat these issues. #argument ","x":-436,"y":1106,"width":356,"height":429},
		{"id":"2e17a2f3c48cc234","type":"text","text":"Often times it's hard to see how architecture can impact our society *here and now*. There is an argument to be made that architecture is weak at leveraging change on a short-term, and the impact only happens over the long-term. Preserving and studying older architecture is undeniably important for historic, cultural and political reasons.\n\nBecause generally [[Architecture only follows|architecture only adapts to]] the changing societal tides, it's interesting when it has a clear direct impact on many people's lives.\n\nThere is an argument to be made, that we can learn from what Hostile Architecture fails at, to adjust how we deal with issues of homelessness, drug- and substance-use, street crime, etc. #argument","x":-436,"y":2181,"width":356,"height":632,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"5b1054debdca7e01","type":"text","text":"#  moving past Hostile Architecture\n\nIs Hostile Architecture a result of a maintained order that should be disposed off?\n\nCan we offer a better alternative? \n\n(How/can/should) do we fight against?#question ","x":-436,"y":2853,"width":356,"height":334,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"be6a491b6646afd9","type":"text","text":"# Rough Sleeper Interview\n\n> [!note] Guidance\n> Maybe skip past the obvious questions, like \"Does Hostile Design lower your quality of life?\". I think there is enough data out there, and whatever I uncover can't compete with large scale surveys. This are much more valuable as accounts of individuals.\n\n","x":-20,"y":-440,"width":420,"height":520},
		{"id":"8ac652a70371c270","type":"text","text":"> [!question] Why is it Hostile Architecture an interesting thing to learn about?\n> Or, why should I [we/architects] care to learn more about it?\n\n","x":82,"y":1524,"width":320,"height":269},
		{"id":"886f495bbac64663","type":"text","text":"Who is most impacted? Benefitted, harmed? #question ","x":82,"y":1840,"width":320,"height":79,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"88960967b8c9d2f7","type":"text","text":"Why is Hostile Architecture such a wide-spread solution? Are there any alternatives and how functional are they?","x":82,"y":1354,"width":320,"height":136,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"d971ae15505c0b85","type":"text","text":"> [!question] What does Hostile Architecture tell/teach us as a society?\n> Yes, *we live in a society*, but really, what *can* we learn about ourselves if we acknowledge the solutions that we implement to address social issues with architecture. #question ","x":82,"y":415,"width":320,"height":351},
		{"id":"7dc0b694cd087c64","type":"text","text":"> [!question] What is Hostile Architecture?\n\n\n\n","x":84,"y":126,"width":320,"height":106},
		{"id":"0a924ad29ca7de22","type":"text","text":"1. Where is its origin?\n2. Where is it most commonly implemented (which countries, neighbourhoods, etc.)","x":82,"y":800,"width":320,"height":175,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"94f7a2761cfa6c81","type":"text","text":"> [!question] WHEN?\n> When did Hostile Architecture become necessary? When did was it \"invented\" / \"discovered\"?\n> \n> Is it needed because we are an urban species?\n> #question ","x":82,"y":994,"width":320,"height":326},
		{"id":"57196e3df36ed405","type":"text","text":"> The use of injection drugs in public spaces is associated with individual (physical, psychological) and societal harms. Physical harms, such as abscess and damage to veins, become more likely in settings where injecting is rushed and hygiene is compromised[[1](https://harmreductionjournal.biomedcentral.com/articles/10.1186/1477-7517-10-22#ref-CR1 \"DeBeck K, Small W, Wood E, Li K, Montaner J, Kerr T: Public injecting among a cohort of injecting drug users in Vancouver, Canada. J Epidemiol Community Health. 2009, 63 (1): 81-6. 10.1136/jech.2007.069013.\")–[8](https://harmreductionjournal.biomedcentral.com/articles/10.1186/1477-7517-10-22#ref-CR8 \"Bourgois P: The moral economies of homeless heroin addicts: confronting ethnography, HIV risk, and everyday violence in San Francisco shooting encampments. Subst Use Misuse. 1998, 33 (11): 2323-51. 10.3109/10826089809056260.\")]. Lack of privacy compounds the shame that many people who use drugs feel[[9](https://harmreductionjournal.biomedcentral.com/articles/10.1186/1477-7517-10-22#ref-CR9 \"Rhodes T, Kimber J, Small W, Fitzgerald J, Kerr T, Hickman M, Holloway G: Public injecting and the need for 'safer environment interventions' in the reduction of drug-related harm. Addiction. 2006, 101 (10): 1384-93. 10.1111/j.1360-0443.2006.01556.x.\"), [10](https://harmreductionjournal.biomedcentral.com/articles/10.1186/1477-7517-10-22#ref-CR10 \"Rhodes T, Watts L, Davies S, Martin A, Smith J, Clark D: Risk, shame and the public injector: a qualitative study of drug injecting in South Wales. Soc Sci Med. 2007, 65 (3): 572-85. 10.1016/j.socscimed.2007.03.033.\")] and communities where public drug use occurs become marked and subject to ==stigma==[[11](https://harmreductionjournal.biomedcentral.com/articles/10.1186/1477-7517-10-22#ref-CR11 \"Tempalski B, McQuie H: Drugscapes and the role of place and space in injection drug use-related HIV risk environments. Int J Drug Policy. 2009, 20 (1): 4-13. 10.1016/j.drugpo.2008.02.002.\")–[13](https://harmreductionjournal.biomedcentral.com/articles/10.1186/1477-7517-10-22#ref-CR13 \"Taylor A, Cusick L, Kinder J, Hickman M, Rhodes T: The social impact of public injecting. Report of the Independent Working Group on Drug Consumption Rooms. 2006, York UK: Joseph Rowntree Foundation\")].^[[A qualitative study of the perceived effects of blue lights in washrooms on people who use injection drugs | Harm Reduction Journal | Full Text (biomedcentral.com)](https://harmreductionjournal.biomedcentral.com/articles/10.1186/1477-7517-10-22)]","x":213,"y":2160,"width":379,"height":429},
		{"id":"6319dcc2f019652e","type":"text","text":"Research conducted by Crisis in England and Wales revealed that six in ten homeless people reported an increase in defensive architecture over the last year, making it impossible for them to find a place to rest. ^[[Copilot](https://copilot.microsoft.com/?FORM=undexpand&) - https://www.crisis.org.uk/about-us/latest-news/new-research-from-crisis-uncovers-dehumanising-effects-of-defensive-architecture/]","x":523,"y":-209,"width":319,"height":212},
		{"id":"62780d1cdf9a3aba","type":"text","text":"Dehumanization of the most vulnerable.","x":557,"y":120,"width":250,"height":70,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"6402fa2f5d4f0f40","type":"text","text":"\"defensive architecture\"","x":557,"y":206,"width":250,"height":60,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"2d64f38cf00e82c9","type":"text","text":"What are design crimes?","x":559,"y":296,"width":250,"height":60,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"8fa5333daf3f1f1e","type":"text","text":"Examples and Case studies of Hostile Architecture","x":560,"y":391,"width":249,"height":115,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"37e5048e4425edd3","type":"text","text":"**Is it** a widely adopted solution?","x":560,"y":538,"width":250,"height":60,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"4516e3bcd7a766c2","type":"text","text":"In what instances do desingers opt for HA?","x":560,"y":618,"width":250,"height":80,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"d1350c203b194b18","type":"text","text":"Why are old buildings important? #question ","x":590,"y":1354,"width":250,"height":86,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"e880ff704763a9fd","type":"text","text":"Architecture (as a field?) is weak at leveraging change in the short-term, but has impact in the long-term. #argument ","x":590,"y":1460,"width":250,"height":188,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"72403d9f0d473260","type":"text","text":"Is there an economic incentive for design crimes?","x":590,"y":1722,"width":250,"height":97,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"ccd612490662ec5e","type":"text","text":"Understanding the impact of stigma (i.e. public drug use) and what are the solutions? #question ","x":590,"y":1840,"width":250,"height":199,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"22963bc1c37b8a2e","type":"text","text":"> [!note]\n> I think it's a good idea to illustrate the specific examples of Hostile Architecture and explain one-by-one how these fail at solving the issue they set out to address.  \n> A sort of blitz debunking segment.","x":660,"y":-1240,"width":393,"height":279},
		{"id":"5a86ee38b1b7fbc0","type":"text","text":"[Drug overdoses in public bathrooms are common: new tools could prevent harm and improve response (theconversation.com)](https://theconversation.com/drug-overdoses-in-public-bathrooms-are-common-new-tools-could-prevent-harm-and-improve-response-191406)","x":681,"y":2820,"width":480,"height":120},
		{"id":"451a23d4f564c2e1","type":"text","text":"[The Safer Bathroom Toolkit - University of Victoria (uvic.ca)](https://www.uvic.ca/research/centres/cisur/projects/active/projects/safer-bathrooms.php)","x":681,"y":3020,"width":480,"height":80},
		{"id":"4777f2a97015e6f7","type":"text","text":"Are there London firms who are advocating for Design Justice or are fighting against design crimes?","x":935,"y":-80,"width":251,"height":165,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"58c056c47aab4385","type":"text","text":"> [!note]\n> The presentation format could be a quiz. \"Works or Shite?\" I present an example and the audience picks which, after which I reveal the correct answer - which inevitably will be Shite for all of them.\n\n> \"I must admit, I rigged this quiz. They are all... \\*sigh\\* ...shite.\"","x":1100,"y":-1240,"width":393,"height":347},
		{"id":"76e6ca8f0c8f396e","type":"text","text":"What is Design Justice","x":1282,"y":-276,"width":250,"height":60,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"97649f631462385d","type":"text","text":"Examples of Design Justice implementations","x":1281,"y":-173,"width":250,"height":60,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"a1da32095c771543","type":"text","text":"###### Examples of Hostile Architecture Implemented\n- spikes\n- curved benches\n- gated doorways\n- deliberate noise pollution\n- wardens, security officers\n- blue lights in bathrooms","x":1282,"y":206,"width":280,"height":494,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"2e48603554656d43","type":"text","text":"How much money, time and effort is spent on the implementation of design injustice? What if spending could be reallocated into developing of non-band-aid solutions.","x":1280,"y":930,"width":312,"height":272,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"027ad57bed214595","type":"text","text":"Has government done anything to regulate design crimes and what constitutes ethical design. Is there legislation to prevent harmful design?","x":1280,"y":1320,"width":316,"height":214,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"0e3821e89e71edfb","type":"text","text":"# Blue lights in washrooms\n\nUse the blue smart bulb to light a bathroom and when presenting this segment. From the direction and cinematography point of view that's quite fun, but also it helps illustrate how hard it is to see in this light.","x":1532,"y":-1240,"width":318,"height":313},
		{"id":"29a62efe2f55be91","type":"file","file":"Thoughts/37 Interests/2 Writing/Blog/Drafts/Hostile Architecture/mini-essays/Hostile Architecture mini-essays.md","x":1580,"y":-440,"width":484,"height":857,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"347e3e9990cafa62","type":"text","text":"Hostile Architecture is a band-aid solution","x":1737,"y":950,"width":250,"height":132,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"2126388738fbcdbb","type":"text","text":"> [!note]\n> \"Okay, so all hostile architecture - bad. What do we do then, smartass?\" you might say, and I reply \"Watch your tone, (*and language*) first of all. And secondly, I will show you better alternatives.\" ","x":1911,"y":-1240,"width":393,"height":279},
		{"id":"b6ab918b5d25cba7","type":"text","text":"> [!question] What are the locations of choice for drug-users?\n\nIn New York City [two-thirds](https://hepfree.nyc/wp-content/uploads/2016/09/IDUHA-Citywide-Study-Report-2015-3.pdf?x41468) of drug users visit places like abandoned buildings, cars, and public bathrooms to inject drugs.","x":2663,"y":667,"width":250,"height":406},
		{"id":"b39e4e6f98c05e75","type":"text","text":"Does installing blue lights in bathrooms create a surge of drug usage in public spaces, since a portion of drug users are suddenly displaced from their usual more private spot in a bathroom stall? #argument ","x":2652,"y":257,"width":269,"height":307,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"23713dc567d6df73","type":"text","text":"![[Hostile Architecture - Blue lights in bathrooms.jpg]]\n\nBlue or purple lights are installed in washrooms to dissuade drug use. Supposedly, the colour of light makes it harder to find superficial veins. This is a common practice in Canada, however, ti has been shown to not work as a dissuasive tactic.^[[BCCDC_Blue_Lights_ Position Statement.pdf](http://www.bccdc.ca/resource-gallery/Documents/Educational%20Materials/Epid/Other/BCCDC_Blue_Lights_%20Position%20Statement.pdf?utm_source=vancouver%20is%20awesome&utm_campaign=vancouver%20is%20awesome%3A%20outbound&utm_medium=referral)]\n\nThe BCCDC Blue Lights Statement says that, although blue lights are bad at discouraging and increase risks associated with injecting drug use. It *is* harder to see veins but people with no other alternative will opt to inject by guessing, which increases the risk of inadvertent self-harm. The poor lighting leads to:\n- injecting into surrounding tissue or arteries\n- trouble measuring the dosage and leading to overdose\n- increased risk of blood borne virus transmission as it's harder to see if the surface of the skin is clean\n\nBecause blue lights aren't addressing the core issue, it is simply displacing the people out of the bathrooms into other areas. This leads to increase in drug use in stairwells, parking areas, alleys, parks, etc. Drug use in public areas increases the risk both for by-standers and the drug users, *and* it changes the preconceptions of public safety.^[My guess would be that suddenly installing a lot of blue lights creates a surge of drug-use in public areas, and creates a domino-effect of increasing risks.]\n\n> Businesses or other organizations with concerns about drug use in their washrooms are encouraged to connect with their local harm reduction coordinator for support in implementing best practice strategies for safer washrooms. This includes installing sharps disposal boxes in all washrooms, which decreases risks associated with inappropriately discarded needles.\n\n[Why are lights in public bathrooms blue or purple? - Vancouver Is Awesome](https://www.vancouverisawesome.com/local-news/heres-why-you-see-blue-or-purple-lights-in-some-vancouver-public-washrooms-4491662)","x":2100,"y":-287,"width":501,"height":1680},
		{"id":"040cf1f57dad2f00","type":"text","text":"> With temperatures plummeting and rough sleeping continuing to rise across the country, Crisis is calling on those designing homeless people out of public spaces to instead work with local services to help homeless people access the help they need.^[[The dehumanising effects of defensive architecture (crisis.org.uk)](https://www.crisis.org.uk/about-us/latest-news/new-research-from-crisis-uncovers-dehumanising-effects-of-defensive-architecture/)]\n\nJon Sparkes, Chief Executive of Crisis, said:\n\n> “Helping people to stay off the streets and rebuild their lives is about basic social justice – it’s the right thing to do. That’s why the ==Homelessness Reduction Bill==, which is currently [in 2016] making its way through parliament, is so urgently needed. If passed, this crucial bill will help prevent people from becoming homeless, instead of being forced to live on the ever-more hostile streets.”","x":2263,"y":1578,"width":464,"height":535},
		{"id":"b72fad9c33da60cd","type":"text","text":"![[Hostile Architecture - Curved benches 2.webp]]\n\n[10 of the Most Inhumane Hostile Architecture Examples - Arch2O.com](https://www.arch2o.com/10-inhumane-hostile-architecture-examples/)","x":2674,"y":-97,"width":412,"height":280},
		{"id":"c1c677204dc27809","type":"file","file":"00 Meta/01 Attachments/Hostile Architecture - New York Leaning Bars ©BertJPDXBKLN on Reddit.webp","x":3521,"y":-68,"width":249,"height":332},
		{"id":"5dc794e65dd36cba","type":"text","text":"[15 Examples of Hostile Architecture around the World - RTF (re-thinkingthefuture.com)](https://www.re-thinkingthefuture.com/designing-for-typologies/a2564-15-examples-of-hostile-architecture-around-the-world/)","x":3521,"y":-203,"width":498,"height":74},
		{"id":"7dcae4347ede1d6b","type":"text","text":"## A self-inflicted wound\n\n> We find ourselves in the middle of an opioid epidemic that is the worst drug crisis in American history. It is a self-inflicted wound and one that is predominantly made in America, as we consume [more than 80% of the world’s supply of opioid pain pills,](https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/20859312) even though we are less than 5% of the world’s population. Many of those prescriptions have led to heroin use; [three out of four](https://www.cdc.gov/drugoverdose/opioids/heroin.html) new heroin users started with prescription narcotics.","x":4880,"y":4070,"width":419,"height":379},
		{"id":"922774123b9bdbb9","type":"text","text":"What prescription narcotics lead to opioid addiction?\n#question #drugs ","x":5403,"y":4210,"width":277,"height":133,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"84b6f8fd1531ede5","type":"text","text":"> And there is no single strategy, as provocative or revolutionary as it may be, that is going to be the answer to turning those numbers completely around. Not even a controversial bathroom in the Washington Heights Corner Project could possibly do that, but in the meantime, Mata and others will work on saving as many lives as possible.","x":4880,"y":4503,"width":419,"height":280},
		{"id":"baa7732c1e0c8bd9","type":"text","text":"The safe injection sites don't promote drug use because they don't sell the drugs, but only provide safety measures to prevent overdoses and aide in recovering from addiction? #argument ","x":5373,"y":3534,"width":280,"height":269,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"a2ad6e5566cdffe3","type":"text","text":"What is the criminalization vs. rehabilitation debate? #question ","x":5373,"y":3017,"width":250,"height":145,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"1ef03781f0b32463","type":"text","text":"> Another ==commonly held belief is that a safe injection site would implicitly condone the use of drugs== and lead to increased use. And yet we now know that theory ==starts to fray when we look at what has happened at the Corner Project== and at a place called InSite in Vancouver, Canada, which Liz Evans also helped found.","x":4880,"y":3274,"width":419,"height":260},
		{"id":"b7aef5c3ea182ae7","type":"text","text":"> [InSite](http://www.vch.ca/public-health/harm-reduction/supervised-injection-sites) started as a pilot project in 2003 and was the first legal supervised drug injection site in North America. To help manage the opioid crisis in Canada, Health Canada [has approved more sites](https://www.canada.ca/en/health-canada/services/substance-abuse/supervised-consumption-sites/status-application.html#app) in recent months.","x":4880,"y":3574,"width":419,"height":160},
		{"id":"3ed7c8c9d8f90569","type":"text","text":"> Though ==the centers don’t provide any illicit drugs, the medical staff are there to provide first aid, including naloxone for overdoses, addiction counseling and mental health assistance==. InSite has seen more than 3 million people since it opened, treated over 6,000 overdoses and [not had a single person die.](http://www.vch.ca/public-health/harm-reduction/supervised-injection-sites/insite-user-statistics)","x":4880,"y":3774,"width":419,"height":260},
		{"id":"27f0dbf3a1864236","type":"text","text":"# social class and capital\n\n> [!info] Definition\n> **Cultural capital** refers to skills, interests, knowledge and behaviours that are considered \"desirable\" or necessary to succeed in life^[I argue that value is \"defined\" by the higher class / those in power / those you'd want to impress]. Put in other words, someone who is seen as \"cultured\", sophisticated or highbrow has a lot of cultural capital.^[I argue that values within this framework depend on context and shifts over time. #argument]\n> \n> **Social capital** refers to the social network that are necessary or beneficial to achieving success. Simply put, being a nepo-baby, or knowing the right people will take you far in an industry that's subject to Social Capital.\n\nAccording to Bourdieu behaving accordingly - having the \"correct\" tastes and qualifications (cultural capital) - and knowing the right people (social capital) just like the hierarchical social class create a sense of shared identities, but also become key sources of inequality between classes.\n\n**Cultural Capital** has three characteristics. It can be:\n1. embodied (i.e. posh accent)\n2. objectified (i.e. designer closing)\n3. institutionalised (i.e. a degree from an Ivy League)\n\n","x":5997,"y":1840,"width":443,"height":1101},
		{"id":"e72fadbbaa8dae94","type":"text","text":"[Pierre Bourdieu: Theory, Definitions, & Impact| StudySmarter](https://www.studysmarter.co.uk/explanations/social-studies/famous-sociologists/pierre-bourdieu/)","x":5997,"y":1520,"width":250,"height":196},
		{"id":"347d5a8d12157812","type":"text","text":"# Pierre Bourdieu's famous studies\n\n> [!example] Pre-requiesites:\n> Marx.\n\n1. _The School as a Conservative Force_ (1966)\n2. _Outline of a Theory of Practice_ (1977)\n3. _Reproduction in Education, Society, and Culture_ (1977)\n4. _Distinction: A Social Critique of the Judgment of Taste_ (1984)\n5. \"Forms of Capital\" (1986)\n6. _Language and Symbolic Power_ (1991)\n","x":6021,"y":1040,"width":620,"height":400,"color":"3"},
		{"id":"1223d784e29c6ced","type":"text","text":"# symbolic violence","x":7440,"y":1840,"width":443,"height":1101,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"c70974ab59aaae97","type":"text","text":"# habitus\n\nThe term refers to the **embodied** aspect of **cultural capital** - habits, skills, dispositions, interests that an individual accumulates over their life.\n\nHabitus can be understood as our established patterns of behaviour. A person's habitus is how they would react in a particular situation, and the way they react *normally* is formed by how they had reacted *in the past*.\n\n> [!example]\n> Imagine a person who has grown up in an upper-class household, and a rich neighbourhood, but have lost their [access to] wealth, and now must fend for themselves, and survive with what they can afford.  \n> Their habitus of living a lifestyle of luxury may cause difficulties in matching their resources with their affordances. They might have to learn new skills and learn to temper their expectations. The mismatch between their habitus and the hostile environment may prevent them from thriving unless/until they learn to adapt.\n\nTaste and preference are also a part of the inherited (embodied) cultural habitus. In other words, taste is a learned behaviour and not innate. Suggesting that taste is a natural preference can be used to further justify social inequalities, by assuming that some people are pre-determined to have a higher cultural capital.","x":6480,"y":1840,"width":443,"height":1101},
		{"id":"684bea6bebaa09a6","type":"text","text":"\n\n> [!bug] Omg, i'm going down a sociology rabbit whole here.\n> okay. if you keep this in the final edit use images to explain this one. if you must include this, illustrate it so it's understood simply","x":6667,"y":1160,"width":353,"height":280,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"02ec301128db6c6d","type":"text","text":"# society and fields","x":6960,"y":1840,"width":443,"height":1101},
		{"id":"48bd074cf61e4b03","type":"file","file":"00 Meta/01 Attachments/Hostile Architecture - woman in blue-lights bathroom ©Private Officer Magazine.webp","x":3819,"y":-68,"width":399,"height":275},
		{"id":"8be2a9ef55ec9259","type":"file","file":"00 Meta/01 Attachments/Hostile Architecture - Under-road spikes.webp","x":3819,"y":264,"width":400,"height":238},
		{"id":"f2d0ab71c640ad0a","type":"text","text":"# Fluorescent blue lights, injecting drug use and related health risk in public conveniences: Findings from a qualitative study of micro-injecting environments\n\n> These findings are discussed within the interpretative frameworks of ==Pierre Bourdieu's theory of habitus and symbolic violence==. The authors conclude that fluorescent blue lights contribute towards the development of situated resistance by injecting drug users within a public injecting habitus; a resistance that produces and reproduces drug-related harm and is a behaviour that opposes the symbolic violence of harm reduction intervention. The paper concludes with suggestions for theory-driven practical intervention that may seek to disrupt the harmful elements of the public injecting habitus.\n\n\n\n\n---\nhttps://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S1353829210000080?via%3Dihub\n\n","x":5000,"y":1407,"width":400,"height":793},
		{"id":"21d7b695e734a691","type":"text","text":"Pierre Bourdieu's theory of habitus and symbolic violence. #question ","x":5440,"y":1600,"width":250,"height":118,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"af23f10139aa9dab","type":"text","text":"What countries implement blue lights?","x":5000,"y":260,"width":249,"height":110,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"b504a8874676f575","type":"text","text":"Most are in Canada, uncommon otherwise.","x":5378,"y":260,"width":238,"height":90},
		{"id":"6a2bbacd257f995b","type":"text","text":"a Gas Station in Pensylvania[^1]\n\n[^1]: [Retailers Are Installing Blue Lights In Bathrooms to Stop Drug Use | Fortune](https://fortune.com/2018/06/25/retailers-blue-lights-bathrooms-opiods/)","x":5378,"y":417,"width":238,"height":297},
		{"id":"725ac671f4d18df3","type":"text","text":"# Blue Lights don't work\n\nBlue lights are effective at discouraging drug-users, instead putting them in more harm.\n\nParticipants of a study done by Harm Reduction Journal[^1] explain the rationale behind their decision making.\n\n1. Drug-users opt for private spaces, but a more immediate solution (such as a public bathroom) override other considerations.\n2. The lighting does make injecting more difficult, yet it doesn't deter everyone from making an attempt. Those who aren't deterred, and inject despite poor visibility face higher risks compared to injecting in well- and neutrally-lit bathrooms.[^2]\n3. Those who are deterred become displaced to more public and visible areas, where they become subject to stigma.\n\n## Conclusions\n\n> We attempt to reconcile this apparent contradiction by interpreting blue lights as a form of ==symbolic violence== and suggest a parallel with other emancipatory movements for inspiration in advocating against this and other oppressive interventions.\n\n\n[^1]: [A qualitative study of the perceived effects of blue lights in washrooms on people who use injection drugs | Harm Reduction Journal | Full Text (biomedcentral.com)](https://harmreductionjournal.biomedcentral.com/articles/10.1186/1477-7517-10-22)\n[^2]: [Fluorescent blue lights, injecting drug use and related health risk in public conveniences: Findings from a qualitative study of micro-injecting environments - ScienceDirect](https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S1353829210000080?via%3Dihub)","x":4547,"y":-23,"width":394,"height":1383,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"3f398ca17aa421ab","type":"text","text":"What are “consumption room,” or safe injection sites? (US) #question ","x":4679,"y":-194,"width":262,"height":126,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"d62fd5225149484f","type":"text","text":"Poor use of space.","x":4297,"y":720,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"2403d341adb54cec","type":"text","text":"## \"moral obligation\" - Corner Project\n\nThe Corner Project had a lot of issues with drug-users and they could have taken measures to prohibit this on their property, however, they understood that doing so will not reduce drug use, but displace it to other, potentially *more* unsafe places. Instead they implemented measures to reduce overdoses in their restrooms.  \nSince, New York State Department of Health has followed their lead and instituted [regulations and recommended procedures](https://www.health.ny.gov/diseases/aids/consumers/prevention/needles_syringes/syringe_exchange/docs/policies_and_procedures.pdf) on overdose prevention.","x":4880,"y":2460,"width":419,"height":433},
		{"id":"dd29c10d94fac62c","type":"text","text":"![[Hostile Architecture - Curved benches.webp]]\n\n[The rise of hostile architecture | CNN](https://edition.cnn.com/2017/11/28/world/gallery/hostile-architecture/index.html)","x":2961,"y":257,"width":412,"height":330},
		{"id":"32713cf292e5febc","type":"text","text":"# Homelessness Reduction Bill [2016]","x":2211,"y":2920,"width":296,"height":80},
		{"id":"ad0a3a9a7267fcb3","type":"text","text":"# Crisis (charity?)","x":2382,"y":2600,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"21bd1732f7911094","type":"text","text":"# Rights Guide for Rough Sleepers","x":2573,"y":2920,"width":250,"height":80},
		{"id":"0459c78ff576b705","type":"text","text":"# Housing Justice (Charity)","x":2848,"y":2713,"width":250,"height":80},
		{"id":"6c602cd7ab42b235","type":"text","text":"# Liberty (Charity)","x":3135,"y":2713,"width":250,"height":80},
		{"id":"6bd683cc80316dee","type":"text","text":"###### Deliberate Noise Polution\n\n> When they did find a place to rest, a fifth (20%) of those surveyed [in 2016] reported suffering from deliberate noise pollution such as loud music or recorded bird song and traffic sounds, making it hard or impossible to sleep.^[[The dehumanising effects of defensive architecture (crisis.org.uk)](https://www.crisis.org.uk/about-us/latest-news/new-research-from-crisis-uncovers-dehumanising-effects-of-defensive-architecture/)]\n","x":2961,"y":612,"width":412,"height":241},
		{"id":"521811c590b7503c","type":"text","text":"## criminalization vs. rehabilitation\n\n> In [Colorado,](http://samhda.s3-us-gov-west-1.amazonaws.com/s3fs-public/field-uploads/2k15StateFiles/NSDUHsaeShortTermCHG2015.htm) which legalized recreational pot, teen marijuana use has dropped. \n> \n> When it comes to opioids, the number of people who overdose and die from legal prescription painkillers is about [equal](https://www.cdc.gov/drugoverdose/data/index.html) to the number of people who die from illicit drugs like heroin.\n\n\n\n","x":4880,"y":2929,"width":419,"height":320},
		{"id":"db6e0c9d7d80a935","type":"text","text":"Are there examples of truly **good** Hostile Architecture?\n\nA solution that limits the end-users control over the environment without harming them? Or a solution that excludes someone in a way that is beneficial to them?","x":935,"y":480,"width":279,"height":308,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"83830463b70f5893","type":"text","text":"Does animal-targetted Hostile Architecture work?","x":946,"y":868,"width":250,"height":112,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"cf1ad0c93f8580f6","type":"text","text":"What are some other examples of design crimes?","x":935,"y":146,"width":250,"height":99,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"0a3e681e524187ac","type":"file","file":"00 Meta/01 Attachments/UVIC Safer Bathroom Toolkit - Design Fact Sheet.pdf","x":682,"y":3180,"width":679,"height":560},
		{"id":"0590d2efb20110f8","type":"file","file":"00 Meta/01 Attachments/UVIC Safer Bathroom - single stall.avif","x":1401,"y":3340,"width":400,"height":240},
		{"id":"7bf331fec5f60588","type":"text","text":"The Safer Bathroom toolkit includes an architectural design fact sheet, which identifies safety and overdose prevention features to consider when building or modifying bathrooms. (Hannah Leyland, Intern Architect)","x":1401,"y":3620,"width":400,"height":200},
		{"id":"0a2a5cce501049ce","type":"text","text":"# Alternatives","x":1361,"y":2467,"width":228,"height":86,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"0a8c0991479fe57a","type":"text","text":"# Wetting down homeless\n\n##### [BBC News - Politics Show - 'Wetting down' London's homeless](http://news.bbc.co.uk/1/hi/programmes/politics_show/regions/london/8408294.stm)\n**Operation Poncho** (2009, London) was supposedly meant as a response to homeless, by the City of London. The advertised goal was to deal with entrenched rough sleepers. Except it looks like the 'dealing' is not by housing people and moving them off the streets but via hostile means like hosing down the homeless.\n\n![[Hostile Architecture - Hosing down homeless.jpg]]\n\nAfter a meeting of all bodies involved and the City of London, they suspended the street cleaning procedures while their effectiveness were under review, however, these were reintroduced, without outside agencies having reviewed it.\n\n> [!important]\n> Charities including =='Housing Justice'== and ==Liberty== have now produced a =='Rights Guide for Rough Sleepers'==.\n\n\n\n","x":2961,"y":1373,"width":560,"height":776},
		{"id":"db6a222c617236f2","type":"text","text":"###### Wardens / Security Officers\n\n> A further 63% had witnessed [in 2016] an increase in wardens and security guards in public spaces. Some respondents reported being regularly ==moved on in the middle of the night==, while 21% reported =='wetting down'== - having makeshift sleeping areas washed down while they were still in them.^[[The dehumanising effects of defensive architecture (crisis.org.uk)](https://www.crisis.org.uk/about-us/latest-news/new-research-from-crisis-uncovers-dehumanising-effects-of-defensive-architecture/)]\n","x":2961,"y":897,"width":412,"height":271}
	],
